<ansi fg="black-bold">.:</ansi> <ansi fg="magenta">Help for </ansi><ansi fg="command">errands</ansi>

A companion you can spare need not idle. From an inn you can send one away
on an errand: an escort, a hunt or a scouting job. They are gone for half
an hour, two hours or eight, in real time, and come home with something
to show for it. They are away from your company
meanwhile, so send only those you can spare.

<ansi fg="yellow-bold">Commands</ansi>

  <ansi fg="command">errands</ansi>                                   who is away and when they are due, who is free to send, and the latest news
  <ansi fg="command">errand send [member] [job] [length]</ansi>       send a companion away: [job] is escort, hunt or scout, [length] is short, medium or long (short if you leave it out)
  <ansi fg="command">errand recall [member]</ansi>                    call a companion home early, with nothing to show for it

In the web client the Company window has an Errands tab, with a card for
each companion: pick a job and a length and press Send, or Call back. It
fits a phone. You name a companion as you do everywhere else: by name, or by
number as <ansi fg="command">#2</ansi>.

<ansi fg="yellow-bold">Who can go, and from where</ansi>

  - From a room that is an inn, the same rooms where you can rent a bed
    (<ansi fg="command">help inn</ansi>), and not in a fight, on a journey or at a camp rest.
  - The companion must be with you, alive, and not fighting. They leave
    your formation as they go and take the same cell again when they come
    home, if it is still free (<ansi fg="command">help formation</ansi>).
  - Creatures and constructs have no errands to run, and a fallen or
    fled companion is not free.

<ansi fg="yellow-bold">The jobs</ansi>

  escort   walks a caravan down the road: steady pay, and safe for one who matches the zone
  hunt     clears game and vermin off the road: better spoils, and a wound's risk
  scout    reads the country: mostly rumours of lairs, little coin, some risk

<ansi fg="yellow-bold">The lengths</ansi>

  short    half an hour    pay as listed
  medium   two hours       two and a half times the pay
  long     eight hours     six times the pay (so a long errand never pays better by the hour; it is for the gap between your sessions)

An errand runs in real time and carries on while you are away from the
game, so a long one can be sent before you log out. It never moves the
world's clock. A companion who is due home waits until you are online
and not in a fight, a journey or a camp rest, and then rejoins you at once.

<ansi fg="yellow-bold">What they bring back</ansi>

Each errand comes to one thing, decided for good the moment it is sent, so
logging out, a restart or a copyover cannot change it:

  Gold      modest pay: 3 gold times the middle level of the zone's level
            band and 1 for each of the companion's own levels, then the job's
            share (an escort 100%, a hunt 140%, a scout 60%) and the length's.
            A level 8 companion in a 7-9 zone earns about 32 gold for a short escort.
  A find    a small gathered good, counted at its full worth as part of the
            pay, with the rest of the pay in gold. Merchants pay less than
            that worth, so a find is never a way to make money out of goods.
  A rumour  word of a lair in the zone whose master your company has not
            slain, so you know where to look. Scouts hear most.
  A wound   a lasting wound, a fifth of the companion's health held back,
            that heals as any wound does (<ansi fg="command">help wounds</ansi>). A hunt risks 15% and a
            scout 8%; an escort, none. Every level a companion stands
            under the zone's band adds 8% (to at most 60%); a companion above
            the band risks half as much.

The pay follows the zone you send from, so an errand from a deeper zone pays
more. A zone that names no band pays by the companion's own level. They
earn no experience, and nothing here lowers or raises their loyalty.

<ansi fg="yellow-bold">What it does to your company</ansi>

An away companion is gone from everywhere: not in battles, not in camp,
not in your load or carry capacity, not eating or sleeping, not talking or
forming bonds, and it cannot be placed in the formation. Its gear goes with
it and comes back with it. If you dismiss one while it is away, the errand
ends with nothing paid and its gear returns to your cargo as with any
dismissal. Errands are saved with the company, and each return goes in
the chronicle (<ansi fg="command">chronicle errands</ansi>).

See also: <ansi fg="command">help company</ansi>, <ansi fg="command">help formation</ansi>, <ansi fg="command">help inn</ansi>, <ansi fg="command">help wounds</ansi>, <ansi fg="command">help chronicle</ansi>, <ansi fg="command">help separation</ansi>, <ansi fg="command">help webclient</ansi>.
